{
  "title": "A Gallery of Convolutions",
  "intro": "Every signal in the viewer, put to work as an input with a sensible impulse response. Each entry says what the output looks like, where it lives, how big it is, and gives one value that the readout prints, so you can confirm it for yourself.",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "How to use this gallery"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "Each entry below is a ready-set viewer. In every one, press Slide t and watch the bottom-right panel trace the output $y(t)$, then drag Shift t to the value quoted and compare the readout, which prints $y(t)$ to three decimals. Two rules from Properties of Convolution (#/math/convolution/properties) make it possible to say a lot in advance. The output lives where the supports add: if $x$ is non-zero on $[a, b]$ and $h$ on $[c, d]$, then $y$ is non-zero on $[a + c, b + d]$. And the areas multiply: area of $y$ equals area of $x$ times area of $h$. We use them in each entry to predict before we measure."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "The identity: an impulse"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "A unit impulse $\\delta(t)$ has area 1 and does nothing: $x * \\delta = x$. Convolving with $\\delta(t - T)$ copies $x$ and moves it $T$ seconds to the right. The viewer draws the impulse as an arrow of height 1."
    },
    {
      "type": "widget",
      "widget": {
        "type": "convolution-3d",
        "title": "Anything convolved with an impulse is itself",
        "params": [
          {
            "key": "x",
            "label": "Input x(t)",
            "control": "select",
            "options": [
              "pulse, 1 s wide",
              "pulse, 2 s wide",
              "triangle, 2 s wide",
              "ramp, 1 s long",
              "decaying exponential e^(−at)u(t)",
              "damped sine e^(−at)sin(2πt)u(t)",
              "unit step u(t)",
              "Gaussian bump",
              "unit impulse δ(t)",
              "echo δ(t) + 0.5·δ(t − 1.5)"
            ],
            "default": "triangle, 2 s wide"
          },
          {
            "key": "h",
            "label": "Impulse response h(t)",
            "control": "select",
            "options": [
              "pulse, 1 s wide",
              "pulse, 2 s wide",
              "triangle, 2 s wide",
              "ramp, 1 s long",
              "decaying exponential e^(−at)u(t)",
              "damped sine e^(−at)sin(2πt)u(t)",
              "unit step u(t)",
              "Gaussian bump",
              "unit impulse δ(t)",
              "echo δ(t) + 0.5·δ(t − 1.5)"
            ],
            "default": "unit impulse δ(t)"
          },
          {
            "key": "a",
            "label": "Decay rate a (1/s)",
            "control": "range",
            "min": 0.2,
            "max": 3,
            "step": 0.1,
            "default": 1
          },
          {
            "key": "t",
            "label": "Shift t (s)",
            "control": "range",
            "min": -1,
            "max": 5,
            "step": 0.05,
            "default": 0.5
          },
          {
            "key": "slices",
            "label": "3D slices",
            "control": "select",
            "options": [
              "fixed t: flip and slide",
              "fixed τ: echoes of h"
            ],
            "default": "fixed t: flip and slide"
          },
          {
            "key": "azimuth",
            "label": "Turn (degrees)",
            "control": "range",
            "min": -180,
            "max": 180,
            "step": 5,
            "default": -50
          },
          {
            "key": "elevation",
            "label": "Tilt (degrees)",
            "control": "range",
            "min": 5,
            "max": 85,
            "step": 5,
            "default": 30
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "text": "The triangle rises from 0 at $t = 0$ to 1 at $t = 1$ and falls to 0 at $t = 2$. With the impulse as $h$, the product at any $t$ is a single spike at $\\tau = t$ whose height is $x(t)$. At Shift t = 0.5 the readout says y(t) = 0.500, the triangle's own height there; at t = 1 it says 1.000. The output panel is the triangle itself, unchanged, and its support is $[0, 2]$, the sum of $[0, 2]$ and $\\{0\\}$."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Echo: two impulses"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "The echo $\\delta(t) + 0.5\\,\\delta(t - 1.5)$ is two impulses, so $x * h$ is $x(t) + 0.5\\,x(t - 1.5)$: the signal plus a quieter copy 1.5 s later. The area of the echo is $1.5$, so the output area is $1.5$ times the input's."
    },
    {
