    {
      "type": "callout",
      "text": "The bump is centred at $t = 1$ with standard deviation 0.25 s and area $0.25\\sqrt{2\\pi} = 0.6267$. At Shift t = 1, half of the bump is behind $t$: the readout says y(t) = 0.313, half of 0.627. At Shift t = 2 the whole bump has been counted: y(t) = 0.627, and it stays there, as the output panel shows. The steepest part of the S, at $t = 1$, is where the bump is tallest, since the slope of a running total is the input itself."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "The pulse and the exponential: charging"
    },
    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "A 1 s pulse into the decaying exponential $e^{-at}u(t)$ is a switch closing for a second on an RC circuit. While the pulse is on, the output climbs as the circuit charges, $y(t) = (1 - e^{-at})/a$; once it is off, the output decays by $e^{-a(t-1)}$ from its last value."
    },
