    {
      "type": "callout",
      "text": "With $a = 1$ the readout at Shift t = 1 shows y(t) = 0.632, which is $1 - e^{-1}$, the peak of the output. The output has no end: it decays for ever, at $t = 3$ it is $0.632 \\times e^{-2} = 0.086$. Its area is $1 \\times 1/a = 1$, the product of the areas. Raise the decay rate to 3 and the same pulse peaks lower, still at $t = 1$ (the output rises for as long as the pulse is on), at $(1 - e^{-3})/3 = 0.317$, and it gets close to that level well before $t = 1$: a faster-decaying $h$ is a shorter memory and has less area."
    },
