      "text": "The damped sine $e^{-at}\\sin(2\\pi t)u(t)$ is what a bell or a plucked string does after a tap: it rings at 1 Hz, fading at rate $a$. As an impulse response it describes a resonator. A system rings loudest when driven at its own frequency. Feed a 1 Hz damped sine to a 1 Hz resonator, both with $a = 0.2$, and the output is, exactly, $y(t) = \\tfrac12 e^{-at}\\left[\\frac{\\sin 2\\pi t}{2\\pi} - t\\cos 2\\pi t\\right]$, whose envelope $\\tfrac12 t\\,e^{-at}$ grows with time instead of fading: each cycle of the input adds to the ringing."
