    {
      "type": "prose",
      "text": "A short pulse is a very different drive. A 1 s pulse is exactly one period of the 1 Hz resonance, and the positive and negative halves of the sine cancel: with $a = 0.5$, the 1 s pulse gives a ringing output whose size is only $0.038$ at $t = 2$ (the readout shows 0.038). Only the damping, which makes the second half-cycle a little smaller than the first, leaves a remainder, so a drive of exactly one period barely excites the resonator. The Gaussian bump (standard deviation 0.25 s) does better: a 1 Hz wave averaged over the bump keeps $e^{-(2\\pi \\cdot 0.25)^2/2} = 29\\%$ of its height, and with the bump as x and $a = 0.5$ the output swings reach about 0.097 after $t = 2$, two and a half times the pulse's 0.038. The frequency view on The Convolution Theorem (#/math/convolution/convolution-theorem) explains all of it: a resonator is a narrow peak in $H(f)$, and what matters is how much of the input's spectrum sits under that peak."
